---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Scroll from '@/components/motion/scenes/Scroll.astro';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="scroll"
  title="Motion: scroll to a target"
  description="scrollTo moves an element into place over a spring, and follows a layout that moves while it runs. The visitor's own scrolling stops it."
>
  <MotionStage scene="scroll" label="Live example: Scroll to a target" height="md">
    <Scroll size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>scrollTo(target, &#123; container, timing, offset, focus &#125;)</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion/scroll</code>,
    scrolls an element into place over a spring. The target is an element or a <code>&#123; x, y &#125;</code> position.
    By default the nearest scrollable ancestor of the element scrolls, or the page; <code>container</code> names another
    scroller.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`import { scrollTo } from '@udixio/motion/scroll';

const { finished } = scrollTo(document.getElementById('pricing')!, { focus: true });
await finished; // 'finished' at the destination, or 'cancelled' when the user scrolled`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>The start edge of the element is brought to the start of the visible area, past the <code>scroll-padding</code> of the scroller and the <code>scroll-margin</code> of the element.</li>
    <li>The destination is read on each frame, so the scroll follows a layout that moves while it runs.</li>
    <li><code>focus: true</code> gives the focus to the target on arrival, without scrolling again. A target that cannot take focus gets <code>tabindex="-1"</code>.</li>
    <li><code>scrollDestination(target, options)</code> reads where the same scroll would stop, without scrolling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The scroll returns <code>&#123; finished, cancel() &#125;</code>. <code>finished</code> resolves to <code>'finished'</code>
    at the destination, or <code>'cancelled'</code>. A wheel turn, a touch, a pointer press or a key press of the user cancels
    it, and so does a new scroll of the same container. While it runs, the <code>overflow-anchor</code> of the scroller is
    <code>none</code>, and it is given back at the end.
  </p>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The function is the same in every framework: the examples keep the target and the box as references, and call
    <code>scrollTo</code> from the handler of a button.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scroll" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'container', values: 'an element, or the window', default: 'the nearest scrollable ancestor, or the page', description: 'The element that scrolls.' },
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.* token', default: 'spring.slow.spatial', description: 'The spring of the scroll.' },
      { name: 'offset', values: '{ x?, y? } in pixels', default: 'none', description: 'Pixels added to the destination on each axis, in scroll units.' },
      { name: 'focus', values: 'true · false', default: 'false', description: 'Gives the focus to the target on arrival, without scrolling again.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The scroll moves on the slow spatial spring unless <code>timing</code> names another.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.slow.spatial']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code>, the scroll jumps to its destination.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Scroll anchoring is off on the scroller while the scroll runs.</li>
    <li>A scroll that is cancelled stays where it stopped: <code>finished</code> then resolves to <code>'cancelled'</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
